air we hold out this candle of hope  in 1989 the communist government caved  to the pressure and agreed to let non  communist parties run in the elections  solidarity and Democratic candidates  went on to win the majority of  Parliament and the presidency they  established the Republic of Poland and  became the first country in Europe to  topple communism the rest of the Soviet  bloc soon followed  [Music]  this posed a challenge for Western  Europe when that union will be a tree  since the end of World War two the  continents democracies had been growing  closer signing free trade deals that  became the precursor to today's European  Union at first the trade deals just  covered coal and steel but as they grew  to include agriculture energy and other  markets more countries joined making  Europe more economically and politically  integrated than it had ever been before  as the former Soviet bloc countries  started establishing democracies of  their own Western Europe needed to find  a way to include them so in 1993 the  established EU countries came up with a  strict checklist for admitting new  members new member states needed to have  a free-market economy  respect human rights and the rule of law  that meant courts had to be independent  and impartial so everyone could get a  fair trial  these EU rules helped bring former  communist countries in while also  keeping them from sliding back into  authoritarianism they were designed to  keep the peace in Europe Poland joined  the EU in 2004 and because of solidarity  success it became one of the most  promising new members each year after it  joined Poland received millions of euros  to help fund highways schools hospitals  and modern infrastructure but 2014  Poland became one of the EU strongest  and most resilient members it even  avoided the recession in 2009 that  crippled economies worldwide polling  showed 72% of poles were satisfied with  EU membership more than any other member  state and Poland's charismatic pro EU  Prime Minister Donald Tusk was chosen to  become the president of the EU European  Council  but in his absence leaders with a very  different vision for Poland's future  rose to power in 2015 peace Poland's  right-wing party shocked the world and  won an absolutely  in the parliament since 2001 peace had  been led by former solidarity leaders  lack and yaroslav coach in ski who felt  that poland's center-left parties had  become elitist and corrupt it appealed  to poland's conservative population in  rural areas peace only earned 38% of the  vote in 2015 on par with previous  election results but after tests left  for the EU his coalition fell apart  making room for the right-wing party to  take over  according to peace after decades of  Soviet control Poland was now being  controlled by the EU so when it came to  power it pledged to take back Poland's  independence legally or illegally the  party already controlled both houses of  parliament and the presidency so it  initiated a hostile takeover of the  judicial branch first to pack this  constitutional court with loyal judges  and then forced out more than a third of  the judges in this other court both acts  were illegal under the Polish  Constitution but the peace majority  Parliament and presidency signed them  into law anyway the party also fired  over 11,000 civil service workers and at  least 280 military officers and it  started levelling fines on news  organizations when it didn't like their  coverage meanwhile has been using Polish  nationalisms justify these moves  remember this 2017 controversial March a  peace politician called it a beautiful  sight while the party cracked down on  protests against their authoritarian  moves as peace tightened control on  Poland the EU issued several warnings to  stop it from breaking its rules but  peace ignored them  it is with a heavy heart that we have  decided to initiate article 7 1 but the  facts leave us no choice  in December 2017 the EU invoked article  7 its nuclear option for the first time  in its history it allows the EU to strip  a member of its voting rights if it  feels it's breaking their criteria  established in 1993 a significant  numbers of laws have been adopted 13 in  total which put at serious risk the  Independence of the judiciary and the  separation of powers in Poland the  problem for the EU is that article 7  isn't going to work the punishment  requires a unanimous vote by all EU  countries and there's one country that  has sworn to protect Poland Hungary  another former Soviet bloc country has  taken a turn towards authoritarianism  under President Viktor Orban and he  vowed to veto any punishment against  Poland pieces proving that it's possible  to reap the economic benefits of EU  membership while flouting the rule of  law and that has sparked an existential  crisis for the EU the rules that were  written to prevent authoritarianism from  ever reappearing in Europe aren't being  followed and there's not much I can do  about it  the you could try cutting off Poland's  funding but it's unclear how that would  pass so for now the EU remains in a  crisis for decades has tried to keep  Europe Democratic but Poland once used  most promising new addition is  threatening to undermine the whole thing  [Music]  [Music]

